package com.example.securitydemo.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.Customizer; // For "withDefaults()"
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.UserDetails;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.UserDetailsService;
import org.springframework.security.provisioning.InMemoryUserDetailsManager;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    // PART 1: The Filter Chain (The Rules)
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/public").permitAll()  // Rule 1
                .anyRequest().authenticated()            // Rule 2
            )
            .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults())        // Rule 3
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());       // Rule 4

        return http.build();
    }

    // PART 2: The User Manager (The Identity)
    @Bean
    public UserDetailsService userDetailsService() {
        UserDetails user1 = User.withDefaultPasswordEncoder() // UNSAFE (Demo only)
            .username("adesh")
            .password("12345")
            .roles("USER")
            .build();

        return new InMemoryUserDetailsManager(user1);
    }
}

(Note: You need to add a @GetMapping("/public") to your Controller to test the public part!)
[6. Line-by-Line Code Explanation]
Pay close attention. This is the syntax you will use in 99% of your projects (Spring Security 6+ / Spring Boot 3+).
The Class Setup
 * @Configuration: Tells Spring "This class contains instructions on how to build Beans." It replaces XML configuration.
 * @EnableWebSecurity: This is the master switch. It tells Spring, "Don't use your default security setup anymore. Use the configuration defined in this class."
PART 1: securityFilterChain (The Rules)
 * @Bean: We are creating an object that Spring will manage. The type is SecurityFilterChain.
 * HttpSecurity http: This is the Builder Object. Think of it as a blank form where you check boxes to enable security features.
The Lambda DSL (auth -> auth...):
5.  .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> ...): We are starting the authorization rules section. "For these HTTP requests, apply these rules..."
6.  .requestMatchers("/public").permitAll(): CRITICAL. This says: "If the URL matches /public, let anyone in. Do not check for password."
7.  .anyRequest().authenticated(): The catch-all rule. "For any other URL not mentioned above (like /hello), the user MUST be logged in."
* Note: Order matters! You must list specific public URLs first, then the catch-all rules last.
The Login Types:
8.  .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults()): Enables the HTML Login Page (for browsers).
9.  .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults()): Enables HTTP Basic Auth (where you send username/password in the Header). This is useful for Postman testing.
 * return http.build(): Takes all the rules you set above, compiles them into the actual Filter Chain, and returns it to Spring.
PART 2: userDetailsService (The User)
 * UserDetailsService: This is an interface that loads user data.
 * User.withDefaultPasswordEncoder(): WARNING. This is deprecated and unsafe for production because it doesn't encrypt passwords properly. We use it only for this specific learning step to keep the code simple.
 * .username("adesh").password("12345"): We are manually creating a user object in Java memory.
 * return new InMemoryUserDetailsManager(user1): This implementation stores the user in the RAM (Random Access Memory). If you restart the server, the user is reset.
[7. Key Takeaways]
 * Control: The SecurityFilterChain bean gives you granular control over every URL.
 * Order Matters: Always define your "Public" (permitAll) rules before your "Private" (authenticated) rules.
 * Separation: We separated the "Rules" (Filter Chain) from the "Data" (User Details).
 * No More Random Passwords: By defining UserDetailsService, the random console password disappears. You can now login with adesh / 12345.
